1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
One sky. Ancient China counted five
sacred beasts. One vanished.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,000
China’s Four Symbols: one sacred beast
for each direction.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
But the centre is empty.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
An ancient text, Da Dai Liji: five
animal groups, five chiefs.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Feathered: phoenix. Furred: qilin.
Shelled: sacred tortoise. Scaled:
jiaolong dragon.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,500
The fifth: “naked” — no feathers or
scales. Chief: the sage, a human.

7
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:34,500
Dragon, bird and tortoise match. No
tiger — the qilin instead. And one
extra: a human.

8
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:41,500
The Book of Rites gives the centre to
the Yellow Emperor and “naked”
creatures. That means humans.

9
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,000
Which stars belong to the centre? Our
club lists three candidates.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,500
One: Xuanyuan. The clue: it was the
Yellow Emperor’s name.

11
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:56,500
A winding chain around Leo: exactly 17
stars. Count them.

12
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:03,500
Two: Vega and Altair (Weaver and
Cowherd) — a rare human story. With
Deneb: the Summer Triangle.

13
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:09,500
Three: Hushi (Bow and Arrow). Five
chariot flags matched stars: four
animals, one human-made bow.

14
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:16,500
The Shiji chronicle: four stars called
the Bow, “facing the Wolf”. It aims at
Sirius.

15
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:24,500
Three candidates, three question marks.
No surviving text says which asterism
belongs to the centre.

16
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:27,500
Orion: three stars the whole world
knows.

17
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,500
Follow for a new sky every week.

